<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#lingua-franca">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingua franca</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A lingua franca is a language used for communication between speakers of different native languages. Akkadian served this role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a> in the trading world of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. English became a global lingua franca largely through the reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lingua-franca.jpg" alt="Lingua franca" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Desconeissut · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linguafranca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term comes from the Mediterranean Lingua Franca, also called Sabir, a pidgin used around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and the eastern Mediterranean as the main language of commerce and diplomacy from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to the 18th century, most notably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. It was a simplified speech based mainly on Italian in the east and Spanish in the west, with many loanwords from Greek, Slavic languages, Arabic and Turkish. Some scholars claim it was only poorly used Italian. Lingua means a language in Italian, and franca means Frankish, since in the late Byzantine Empire Franks was a term for all Western Europeans. The name is first recorded in English in the 1670s, with an earlier use in 1632.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A lingua franca is any language regularly used for communication between people who do not share a native language, whatever its history or structure. It may be a pidgin, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a>, or an established language learned as a second language, such as the colonial languages used between diverse communities in former colonies. Pidgins are rapidly developed and simplified combinations of languages with few or no native speakers, while creoles develop from pidgins into fully complex languages with native speakers. A vernacular is the native language of a geographical community, whereas a lingua franca is used beyond its original community, for trade, religion, politics or scholarship. International auxiliary languages such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esperanto">Esperanto</a> have not been widely adopted and are not described as lingua francas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Since the 16th century, as European colonisation made pidgins widely known, the term has shifted from the name of one language to a general term for pidgins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a> and other vehicular languages. Lingua francas have existed since antiquity. Akkadian served much of Western Asia until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> replaced it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> was adopted in Southeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 1st millennium CE through monks, pilgrims and merchants. Literary Chinese was the written and diplomatic language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> until the early 20th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> spread after the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> remained the language of science and scholarship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until well into the 18th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lingua franca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Royal Road's courier system let the king communicate with the furthest corners of the empire within days, and Imperial Aramaic was the administrative <b>lingua franca</b> across the satrapies whatever the local language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Akkadian language, promoted as the language of royal administration, later became in its Babylonian form the diplomatic <b>lingua franca</b> of the ancient Near East for much of the second millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Nahuatl, already widespread and further spread by Aztec power, became the most important indigenous <b>lingua franca</b> of colonial New Spain, used by missionaries as a principal vehicle for evangelisation across central Mexico.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Hammurabi and his successors, became the classical standard of the language and the basis of the Babylonian that was the diplomatic <b>lingua franca</b> of the Near East for centuries afterwards: Egyptian pharaohs, Hittite kings and the rulers of smaller states corresponded in it, as the Amarna archive demonstrates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The English language, spread by imperial administration, commerce and education, became the world's dominant <b>lingua franca</b> for science, commerce, diplomacy and popular culture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Malay served as the <b>lingua franca</b> of administration and commerce, while Chinese communities dominated retail trade and money-lending.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
